**Eviction in a caching system means removing existing data from the cache to make room for new data when the cache reaches its capacity.** This is managed through *cache eviction policies* that decide which items should be discarded. It can be solved by choosing the right eviction strategy based on system requirements, workload patterns, and performance goals.

---

        ## 🗂️ What is Cache Eviction?
        - **Definition**: Cache eviction is the process of clearing data from a cache when
        it becomes full, so new or more relevant data can be stored.
- **Reason**: Since cache memory is limited, not all data can be stored indefinitely. Eviction ensures
that the cache continues to serve frequently accessed or important data.
- **Analogy**: Think of a cache like a small fridge. When you buy new snacks, you may need to
throw out old ones. The eviction policy decides which snacks to discard.

        ---

        ## ⚙️ Common Cache Eviction Policies
| Policy | How It Works | Best Use Case |
        |--------|--------------|---------------|
        | **LRU (Least Recently Used)** | Removes the item that hasn’t been accessed for the longest time | General-purpose systems where recent access predicts future use |
        | **LFU (Least Frequently Used)** | Removes items accessed the least number of times | Systems with stable access patterns (e.g., popular content caching) |
        | **FIFO (First-In-First-Out)** | Removes the oldest item added to the cache | Simple systems where order matters more than frequency |
        | **MRU (Most Recently Used)** | Removes the most recently accessed item | Rare, but useful in cases where older data is more valuable |
        | **Random Replacement (RR)** | Randomly removes an item | Low-complexity systems where predictability isn’t critical |
        | **TTL (Time-To-Live)** | Removes items after a fixed time duration | Useful for expiring stale data (e.g., session tokens) |
        | **Size-Based Eviction** | Removes items exceeding memory or size limits | Large object caching (images, files) |

